  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t({cos}^{2}\u2061t+{sin}^{2}\u2061t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
